# Configuration for `docker compose up`. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Compose reads `.env` from the directory it runs in, so this is the root one and it configures
# the *container*. It is not the same file as the `.env` beside an agent's manifest: that one is
# the agent's own, and the ambient environment — which is what compose sets — deliberately wins
# over it, so an operator can configure the agent their container runs.
#
# `.env` is gitignored. This file is not, and must never hold a real value.

# **Only one variable is required.** The server comes up hosting *no agents* — the API, the web UI
# and the reference at `/docs` exist before there is anything to talk to, which is what makes
# provisioning through them possible — so a model key configures nothing until an agent exists. The
# `MODEL_*` block below belongs to the mounted-agent shape and is commented out in
# `docker-compose.yml` alongside the mount itself.

# ─── Required ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The API token every route requires, except `/v1/health` and `/v1/ready`.
#
# Not boilerplate. The image binds 0.0.0.0, because a process listening on loopback *inside* a
# container is unreachable through a published port — and a non-loopback bind then requires a
# token by design, so `serve` refuses to start rather than serving the world open. Generate one:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
DISPACH_API_TOKEN=

# ─── For a mounted agent only ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The model. Any OpenAI-compatible `/chat/completions` endpoint.
#
# **Uncomment the `MODEL_*` block in `docker-compose.yml` for these to reach the container at all.**
# They are commented there with the `/agent` mount, because an agent created *inside* the container
# with `dispach init` carries its own `.env` and needs none of this.
#
# The id is not a secret and is written literally in a real manifest — it is a variable here
# because `examples/minimal` exists to demonstrate one manifest against four endpoints, and says
# so in the file. Point these at whatever you have: DeepSeek, OpenAI, an Anthropic-compatible base
# URL, or a host serving open weights.
#
# **Which of these an agent actually uses depends on its manifest.** `examples/minimal` writes
# `${MODEL_ID}`, so all three reach it. `examples/shell-agent` writes the id and base URL
# *literally* — which is what a real manifest does — so only `MODEL_API_KEY` reaches it, and the
# key has to belong to the provider that manifest names. A DeepSeek key against a manifest saying
# OpenAI is a `401` from a container that is otherwise working perfectly.
MODEL_ID=deepseek-v4-flash
MODEL_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com/v1
MODEL_API_KEY=

# ─── Optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Host port. The container always listens on 7420; this is only the published mapping, and it is
# configurable because 7420 is often already taken by a `serve` running outside Docker.
# HOST_PORT=7420

# The agent to run. A directory holding `agent.yaml` and its workspace files, mounted at `/agent`
# **read-write** — the skills cache and `memory_write` both write beside the manifest.
#
# **Nothing is mounted unless you uncomment the mount in `docker-compose.yml`**, so this variable
# alone does nothing. That is deliberate: it used to default to `./examples/minimal`, and a fresh
# clone therefore came up hosting the repository's own example agent.
#
# `./examples/minimal` pins **no tools at all**, which makes it the gentle choice and the wrong
# thing for exercising the shell. `./examples/shell-agent` is `--system full` — `exec`, the file
# tools, `config_set` — and is the one to point this at when the thing being tested is what the
# agent can *do*. Read that example's README first: the containment below is what makes it a
# reasonable thing to run, and none of it applies on a laptop.
# AGENT_DIR=./examples/minimal
